[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is conclusive. No flapping script exists on router6. The system has been running stably for ~7.5 minutes since boot — in a **permanently broken** state. Router6's own routing table shows `172.18.51.0/24` as directly connected (eth1), yet it is never injected into the RIP domain because `network 10.0.0.0/8` only activates RIP on eth2. The "intermittent" window the users observed was the 180-second RIP invalid timer expiring at peer routers after the wrong `network` statement took effect.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** RIP daemon configuration on router6 — specifically the `network` statement in the `router rip` block.

**Mechanism:** RIP activates on an interface only when that interface's IP address falls within a configured `network` prefix. Router6's entire RIP configuration is `network 10.0.0.0/8`, which matches only eth2 (10.50.1.2/24, the uplink to router3). Router6's eth0 (172.18.50.254/24) and eth1 (172.18.51.254/24) — the direct gateway interfaces for Location C's pc15–pc18 subnet — lie in the 172.18.x.x range and are therefore invisible to the RIP process. Router6 KNOWS 172.18.51.0/24 as a locally connected route (confirmed in its FIB) but never sends RIP advertisements for it on any interface. As a result, no other router in the domain ever installs a route to 172.18.51.0/24. Inbound packets destined for Location C are silently dropped at the first router that lacks the prefix (router3, router4, etc.). The "routes aging out and being relearned" experience users reported corresponds to the 180-second RIP invalid timer: once the misconfiguration took effect, peer routers' previously-learned routes to 172.18.51.0/24 expired and were never refreshed.

**Localization:** router6, `router rip` configuration block — missing `network 172.18.0.0/16` (or explicit `network 172.18.50.0/24` and `network 172.18.51.0/24` statements).

**Root cause:** router6 has only `network 10.0.0.0/8` in its RIP configuration, omitting the 172.18.x.x subnets, so Location C's gateway prefixes are never advertised into the RIP domain.

**Fix:** Add `network 172.18.0.0/16` (or the two explicit /24 statements) to router6's `router rip` block. Once added, ripd will send RIP updates for 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 on eth0/eth1, router3 will propagate them to all other routers, and cross-site reachability to Location C will be fully restored within one RIP update cycle (~30 seconds).
=== END ===